    "## What to write in your report from this notebook\n",
    "\n",
    "| Report section | Use this |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Evaluation metrics and results** | Table of accuracy and macro-F1 per model and variant (section 5), confidence intervals (Fig 20) |\n",
    "| **Analysis of performance** | Paired drops with CIs (section 6), per-class recall and confusion matrices under same resolution (Figs 21-22), hard cases (Fig 26) |\n",
    "| **Potential biases / dataset artefacts** | The resolution-shortcut test (cross-group vs within-group error), colour-dependence (grayscale, hue shift), UMAP coloured by size (Fig 25) |\n",
    "| **Statistical rigour** | Paired bootstrap and McNemar with Bonferroni correction (section 7) |\n",
    "| **Retrieval (brief requirement)** | Precision@K tables, retrieval examples (Figs 23-24) |\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to interpret the key tests**\n",
    "- **same_res**: a *large, significant drop* with a rise in cross-group errors means the model used resolution or sharpness as a cue (a shortcut). *No drop* means it learnt content that survives low resolution. Either result is valid; explain it.\n",
    "- **grayscale / hue_shift**: a big drop means heavy reliance on colour. Colour is a legitimate cue for these classes, but it limits how well the model transfers to other sensors, seasons or regions.\n",
    "- **darker / brighter**: sensitivity to illumination and haze.\n",
    "- **Probes vs fine-tuned ViT:** fine-tuning can learn dataset-specific shortcuts that frozen features avoid. Compare the drops, not only the original accuracies.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Ethics link:** a model that works on one sensor and resolution but fails on another is risky if used for environmental monitoring or land-use decisions in regions the training data did not cover. Use your robustness results as evidence in the ethics and societal-implications section.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Limitations to state**\n",
    "- Perturbations are synthetic. Real sensor or season shifts may differ.\n",
    "- The shortcut test shows *sensitivity* to resolution; the cause could also be general loss of detail.\n",
    "- One dataset, 4 coarse classes, one train/val/test split.\n",
    "- The ViT is the single seed chosen by validation score, not all three seeds.\n",
